  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
